  "query_preview": "..Latest IR/WV satellite imagery shows partly to mostly \ncloudy skies (high clouds) for much of southeast Arizona as our area \nresides within southwesterly flow on the east side of a broad trough \naxis that extends from Utah down along the AZ/CA border. This \npattern continues to result in some breezes this afternoon, \nespecially to the south and east of Tucson. Meanwhile, cloud \ncoverage will gradually diminish from west to east tonight into \nThursday morning. \n\nA benign weather pattern will continue Thursday into the weekend. \nThe remnants of the aforementioned trough will shear out Thursday \nwith another weak system taking up residence near northern Baja \nFriday then shifting across Sonora Saturday. This could result in \njust enough mid level moisture for a few build-ups this weekend, \nmainly across the higher terrain south and east of Tucson. \nOtherwise, not much to talk about through the weekend with just a \nfew breezes each afternoon. Temperatures will be just slightly below \nnormal through Saturday, then near normal Sunday. \n\nEnsembles are depicting another trough moving from southern CA into \nAZ on Monday. This will have a bit more in the way of dynamics to \nwork with given its strength and coming in at a negative tilt. The \nbig question is just how much moisture will it be able to pull north \nout ahead of that trough axis and where will it line up. Latest \nindications are a plume of PWAT's in the 0.75\" to 1 inch range will \nmove north into the area mainly from Tucson eastward Monday, and the \ncombination of this moisture and increased lift with the trough will \nbe enough for a 10 to 30 percent chance of showers and thunderstorms \nfrom Tucson eastward. With this trough moving in, breezes will start \nticking a bit higher on Monday. Otherwise, more notable outflow \nwinds could occur with any thunderstorms in this pattern with QPF \nremaining low. \n\nBy Tuesday, as the trough axis continues to shift east, the moisture \nwill also shift east into NM. The trends in the past day have been a \nbit quicker to push the moisture east, but even with that said, \nstill can't rule out a slight chance of showers and thunderstorms \nmainly in the White Mountains. Otherwise, dry conditions Tuesday and \nWednesday with some afternoon breezes. \n\nTemperatures will be near to slightly below normal for Monday \nthrough Wednesday.",
